<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atholl</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Athfotla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Atholl is a historic district of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-highlands">Highlands of Scotland</a>, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a>, covering the valleys of the Tay, Tummel and Garry. Its name, recorded as Athfotla, is usually interpreted as 'New Ireland'. It was a Pictish kingdom with its own kings in the 8th century, later a mormaerdom and an earldom, and since 1703 has given its title to the dukes of Atholl, seated at Blair Castle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atholl.jpg" alt="Atholl" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benson85 at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atholl_28district29-1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Atholl lies in the heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-highlands">Scottish Highlands</a>. In clockwise order from the north-east it borders Marr, Gowrie, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strathearn">Strathearn</a>, Breadalbane, Lochaber and Badenoch. In Scottish Gaelic the name is Athall. The historian James E. Fraser has questioned the reading New Ireland: on the basis of the early spelling Athochlach he proposed ath, 'ford, way through', and fochla, 'north', giving 'way to the north', perhaps a Gaelic equivalent of a Pictish name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Three place names in Atholl, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkeld">Dunkeld</a> and the mountains Schiehallion and Rohallion, preserve the name of the Caledonians, a tribe or confederation recorded by Roman writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>. The first documented record of Atholl itself is an 8th-century mention in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a>. The 9th-century poem Seven Children of Cruithne and the longer Pictish King Lists count it among the seven Pictish territories founded by the eponymous children of Cruithne son of Cinge, the mythical founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>. Apart from Fortriu it is the only one of them documented as having its own king.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> described the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> as having their seats among the mountains, and Atholl may have been their leading royal region before it came under Fortriu during the 8th century. It then became one of the core provinces of the Kingdom of Alba. Its first known mormaer, Dubdon of Atholl, was killed in 965 at Dorsum Crup, in the battle between King Dub and his challenger Cuilén. The first recorded earl, Matad, held the title in the 12th century, and Queen Anne made it a dukedom in 1703.</p><p>The earls' right to hold courts for the district ended with the Heritable Jurisdictions Act of 1746, after which Atholl answered to the sheriff of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a>. In the mid-19th century local government reforms replaced the old provinces with counties aligned to the sheriffdoms, and Atholl became the northern part of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Atholl now forms the northern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> and Kinross. Its towns and villages include Aberfeldy, Ballinluig, Blair Atholl, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkeld">Dunkeld</a>, Kirkmichael, Logierait, Pitlochry and Weem. Alexander Mackenzie, the second Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, was born at Logierait in 1822. The dukedom carries numerous subsidiary titles, among them Marquess of Atholl, created in 1676, and Earl of Strathtay and Strathardle, created in 1703.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Atholl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A later medieval tract names seven provinces, Cait (Caithness), Ce, Fib (Fife), Fidach, Fotla (<b>Atholl</b>), Circinn and Fortriu, and a list of Pictish kings survives in later copies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Gaelic had long been advancing into Pictland: <b>Atholl</b>, meaning New Ireland, is attested in the early 8th century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/picts">Picts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>James I, King of Scots since 1406, was murdered at Perth in a failed coup by his uncle and former ally Walter Stewart, Earl of <b>Atholl</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-21">21 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Atholl</b>, Athfotla, appears in Irish annals from the 8th century, when a king of Atholl was drowned by Oengus son of Fergus, king of the Picts, in 739.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-atholl">Kingdom of Atholl</a></li></ul></section>
